# -*- mode: org -*-
#+TITLE: The queue for the stdcxx revival
#+STARTUP: overview
#+OPTIONS: toc:nil ^:nil
#+TODO: TODO WAITING | DONE

The queue, one chapter per part of the tree, in the order the work is
wanted; within a chapter, priority order top first. Each entry states
the fact, the fix and the check that settles it. A =WAITING= entry is
investigated and reported and waits on its owner; it leaves when the
fix it waits for lands and its check passes. Finished entries leave;
git keeps them. The =analysis-= notes under =doc/notes= carry the
longer investigations.

#+TOC: headlines 2

* Library

The library itself, =include= and =src=: its behaviour, its interface
and what it carries that it no longer uses. The locale entries come
first, the easiest at the top: fixes of a line or two, then fixes that
take work, then the entries that wait on a decision. The extension
sweep's findings follow, the smallest decision first: dead code and
stale comments, then behaviour the library adds or changes, then the
additions whose removal costs tests or callers. The rest, outside the
locale, is last.

** TODO Racing =locale::global= calls can split the C and C++ locales

=locale::global= (=src/locale_global.cpp= 59-72) calls =setlocale= and
swaps the body under two different locks, so two racing calls can
leave the C locale from one and the C++ global from the other.
Hypothesis, by reading, 2026-11-02.

Fix: one lock around both, or a decision that the race is the
caller's.

Check: a =22.locale.statics.mt= variant comparing =setlocale (LC_ALL, 0)=
with =locale ().name ()= after the race.

** TODO =%EY= keeps a year its format then fails on

=__rw_read_era= stores its local =tm= whenever no bit but =eofbit= is
set (=include/loc/_time_get.cc= 301-334). Under =%EY= a =%Ey= of the
era format that succeeds sets the year in the local =tm=; a literal
that then fails, or the end of input, adds only =eofbit=, so the year
read is stored: "Heisei 8 Nex" under the test locale stores 1996 with
=eofbit=. The comment at =include/loc/_time_get.cc= 99-100 and
=doc/notes/analysis-time-get-state.md= 9.2 say a failed era directive
leaves the year as it was; =%EC= and a lone =%Ey= do (=src/era.cpp=
340-373). Found by the documentation sweep of 2026-11-02, by reading.

Fix: a decision: keep the year a =%EY= read before its format failed
(C++11 22.4.5.1.2 p13 leaves the members unspecified), or store
nothing unless the whole format matched. The comment at =include/loc/_time_get.cc= 97-100 and
=analysis-time-get-state.md= 9.2 follow.

Check: a row under the test locale, "Heisei 8 Nex" against =%EY=,
pinning the decision either way.

** TODO =eofbit= before the pattern ends sets =failbit= and stores nothing

"%Y-%m" and "%Y " on "2026" both fail: the walk ends with =failbit=
when a directive sets =eofbit= before the pattern is done. N3242 p8
lists ~err != goodbit~ before ~s == end~, a reading under which
=eofbit= is left alone and the year stored. Found by probe,
2026-11-02.

Fix: a decision on how p8's order reads; then the walk.

Check: the two rows pinning the reading taken.

** TODO Implied =tm_wday= and =tm_yday= are left as the caller had them

"%Y %m %d" determines the weekday and the day of the year; the
library stores neither and glibc's =strptime= fills both. Not a
conformance question (N3242 p12 owes only the directive's members);
a decision on whether to fill them. Found 2026-11-02.

Fix: a decision; if filled, after the walk, from the three fields.

Check: a row "%Y %m %d" on "2026-11-02" with =tm_wday= 0, =tm_yday= 305.

** TODO =%C= of a year past 9999 writes the century modulo 100

Year 10000 writes "00", pinned at =tests/localization/22.locale.time.put.cpp=
708; the POSIX.1-2024 width sentence b54acca9 cites gives "100".
Found 2026-11-02.

Fix: a decision, then the row.

Check: the row at 708 says what the decision says.

** TODO =put= over a pattern pads every directive to the width

The width of the stream is applied to every directive of a pattern
and never reset: "%H:%M" at width 5 writes "***09:***05". No row has
a pattern with a width. Found by probe, 2026-11-02.

Fix: a decision: the width applies once (the first directive, as
=num_put= consumes it) or to each; then reset or keep.

Check: a row with a width and a two-directive pattern.

** TODO The blank bit is visible through =ctype<char>::is= under a database

=plan-localedef-backend.md= 3.1 says the =blank= bit written as 0x400
is "invisible until a =ctype_base::blank= names it", and the header
comment of =util/localedef/wctype.cpp= says it "stays unread". By
probe, under =de_DE.ISO-8859-1= the vector =ctype<char>::is= returns
0x403 for a space (the classic facet 0x003) and =table ()= hands out
the same words; the wide vector form strips it with =__rw_all=. Found
2026-11-02.

Fix: a decision: strip the bit in the narrow vector form and =table ()=
as the wide form does, or name it a visible extension and record it.

Check: the vector =is= of a space under a database locale equals the
classic facet's, or the extension is in the master document.

** TODO Database locales have empty =truename= and =falsename=

=util/localedef/wnumeric.cpp= 94-97 writes both names empty, so under
every database locale =boolalpha= writes nothing and reads nothing
(en_US, de_DE, fr_FR by probe); the C-library path gives "true" and
"false". =tests/localization/22.locale.numpunct.cpp= 223 carries
"FIXME: exercise falsename() and truename() in named locales". Found
2026-11-02.

Fix: a decision on a named locale's names (the C facet's, as the
C-library path does, is the natural one); the writer and a test row
follow.

Check: a =boolalpha= round trip under =en_US.UTF-8= from the database.

** TODO A multibyte separator in a narrow facet comes back as its first byte

A narrow =numpunct= or =moneypunct= separator wider than one byte is
returned as its first byte: under =fr_FR.UTF-8= =num_put<char>= writes
"1\xE2234\xE2567", invalid UTF-8; =fr_CA='s money separator is 0xC2.
=22.locale.numpunct= pins the first-byte reading. 14882:2003 makes the
separator one =char=. Found by probe, 2026-11-02.

Fix: a decision: an ASCII fallback for a narrow separator that does
not fit (what glibc's =localeconv= returns is the datum), or record
the limit.

Check: the narrow output of every UTF-8 locale is valid UTF-8, or
the limit is in the master document.

** TODO A mixed-name global does not reach the C library

=locale::global= calls =setlocale= with the library's composite name,
which lists its six categories; glibc accepts a composite only when it
lists all twelve of its own, so =setlocale (LC_ALL, 0)= stays "C"
after =global= of a mixed-name locale (by probe). The comment at
=src/locale_global.cpp= 61-62 assumes =setlocale= understands the
format; =ref-locale.md= ch. 3 says the call takes effect. 14882:2003
22.1.1.5 p2. Found 2026-11-02.

Fix: a decision: compose glibc's twelve-category name for the call,
or record that a mixed name does not reach the C library.

Check: =setlocale (LC_ALL, 0)= after =global= of a two-locale mixture
names both, or the master document records the limit.

** TODO The UTF-16 names set flags no converter reads

The constructor of =codecvt_byname<wchar_t, char>= (=src/wcodecvt.cpp=
near 1100) recognizes =UTF-16=, =UTF-16BE= and =UTF-16LE= and sets
=__rw_utf16*=, but no converter reads the flags: =out= under
=UTF-16= writes "61 c3 a9", UTF-8. Found by probe, 2026-11-02.

Fix: a decision: implement the three, or stop recognizing the names
so the C library or =error= answers.

Check: per the decision; a row in =22.locale.codecvt= either way.

** TODO ISO-2022 conversion does not convert

By probe: =in= of "a ESC $ B $ \" ESC ( B" under =ISO-2022-JP=
returns =error= at byte 0; =out= of {a, U+3042} writes ESC $ @, the
1978 designation; =unshift= writes nothing; =-KR= and =-CN= answer
=error= and 0 to everything; =do_length= (=src/iso2022.cpp= 1871,
1876) returns =CODECVT_ERROR= (2) as a length when no state slot is
free, and a stray ~while (*from == ESCAPE_CHAR)~ around the escape
branch reads =*from= at =from_end= after an escape that ends the
input. No test covers any of it; the ISO-2022 branches also check no
state (=analysis-codecvt-invalid-state.md= ch. 0 says every path
does). Found 2026-11-02.

Fix: a decision first: keep ISO-2022 and make it work against glibc's
=iconv=, or drop the four names.

Check: a round trip of the test fixtures through =iconv= and the
facet; ASan on =length= over "\x1b(B".

** TODO A corrupt =mbstate_t= on the C-library path could be told from a carried one

The C-library =in= and =length= of the wide byname facet take the
state as the C library takes it, since =mbsinit= is the only
question the C library answers and a carried half character and a
corrupt state both answer no (=analysis-codecvt-corrupt-state.md=
ch. 6). Under glibc the state of a UTF-8 character in progress holds
its pending bytes with their count in the low eight bits of
=__count= and the character's total length above them: a carried
state has a count below its total and a total of two to six, and a
corrupt state does not. glibc's =mbrtowc= does not return from a
corrupt state under UTF-8. Decided 2026-11-04 as a possibility,
after the C-library path was left undefined on a corrupt state.

Fix: a characterization that learns the layout of glibc's
=mbstate_t= for a character in progress, and a check in the two
branches that rejects a state outside it, asserting in a debug
build and returning =error= or 0 otherwise, on glibc only. The
layout is glibc's private business; the characterization is what
would notice it moving.

Check: =22.locale.codecvt='s corrupt-state section run against the
C-library facets again, on pin, with =test_libc_split= still
passing.

** TODO A character given the weights of another compares greater than it

In the test locale of =22.locale.collate=, =<U00E8>= is listed last
in the order with =<a>='s weights, ~<a>;<LETTER>;IGNORE~, and
=compare ("\xc3\xa8", "a")= returns 1 on both the narrow and the wide
facet, where identical weights at every level should give 0. The
third level is ~forward,position~ and both elements are =IGNORE=
there. Seen while the undefined-character check was written,
2026-11-04; the check does not pin it. Hypothesis: the position
level weighs an =IGNORE=d element by its ordinal in the order, or
the database writes a weight for it that is not =<a>='s.

Fix: establish which with =util/locale= on the database, then
either the writer or the position level of =__rw_process_offsets=.

Check: the pair compares 0 on both facets, with a row in
=22.locale.collate=.

** TODO The =__rw_once= comment names a static mutex path that is gone

=src/once.cpp= 95-97 says the static mutex of =int= "is itself
constructed through =__rw_once= where mutexes cannot be initialized
statically". 92d18f73 removed that branch with
=_RWSTD_NO_STATIC_MUTEX_INIT=; =__rw_get_static_mutex= returns the
statically initialized mutex (=include/rw/_mutex.h= 193-198) and
calls no =__rw_once=.

Fix: the comment says what the code does.

Check: reading.

** TODO A width comment in =_ioinsert.cc= predates 69ad31e6

=include/rw/_ioinsert.cc= 128-130 says =__rw_insert= "calls width(0)
if and only if the write has been successful"; 69ad31e6 made the reset
unconditional once the sentry is good, and the comment at 164-165 says
so. Seen in passing by the extension sweep, 2026-11-04.

Fix: the comment says what the code does.

Check: reading.

** TODO An unformatted input function whose sentry fails leaves =gcount ()= as it was

14882:2003 27.6.1.3, p1: an unformatted input function counts the
characters it extracts and, if no exception has been thrown, "ends by
storing the count in a member object"; p2 has =gcount ()= return the
number the last one extracted. The functions that zero the count do so
inside ~if (__ipfx)~ (=include/istream.cc= 215, 304, 375, 410, 570,
674), so one whose sentry fails stores nothing: by reading, ~is.read
(b, 3); is.setstate (std::ios::failbit); is.read (b, 1);~ leaves
=gcount ()= at 3 where p1 stores 0. Seen in passing by the extension
sweep, 2026-11-04.

Fix: each unformatted function zeroes the count before its sentry.

Check: that sequence in =27.istream.unformatted.get= prints 0.

** TODO =include/rw/_typetraits.h= is included by nothing

The header defines type-trait helper templates, and no file in the
tree includes it: =git grep _typetraits= finds only the header itself.
=make install= copies it with the rest of =include=. Seen in passing
by the extension sweep, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: remove the header, or keep it for a later use; for
removal, nothing reads it.

Check: =git grep -n _typetraits= is empty; a sanity build.

** TODO =DIV_OVERLOADS= emits two macros nothing reads

=etc/config/src/DIV_OVERLOADS.cpp= checks =div= on =int=, =long= and
=long long= and emits =_RWSTD_NO_DIV_INT=, =_RWSTD_NO_DIV_LONG= and
=_RWSTD_NO_DIV_LLONG= (=config.h= 123-125 of a 15D build under GCC).
=include/ansi/cstdlib= reads only =_RWSTD_NO_DIV_LONG= (61, 140);
nothing reads the other two, and no ~div (long long)~ or =lldiv_t=
appears in =include=. They are the kind 55ceb9b6 trimmed from
=ABS_OVERLOADS=. Seen in passing by the extension sweep, 2026-11-04.

Fix: the probe checks =long= alone, as 55ceb9b6 did for =abs=; or a
decision to add ~div (long long)~, which chapter 2's "Compiler types
beyond 2003" row then names.

Check: the =config.h= of a 15D build has no =_RWSTD_NO_DIV_INT= or
=_RWSTD_NO_DIV_LLONG= line.

** TODO =basic_filebuf::xsputn= returns -1 on an error

14882:2003 27.5.2.4.5, p2: =xsputn= returns "The number of characters
written". =basic_filebuf::xsputn= returns -1 when the buffer is not
open or not in output mode (=include/fstream.cc= 430-431), and on the
write errors after it (464, 496). A caller that adds the result to a
count goes back by one. Seen in passing by the extension sweep,
2026-11-04.

Fix: the override returns the number of characters it wrote, 0 when it
wrote none.

Check: a =27.filebuf= row: =sputn= on a buffer opened for input only
returns 0.

** TODO =allocator::allocate= does not check its count against =max_size ()=

=include/rw/_allocator.h= 134 passes ~__n * sizeof (value_type)~ to
=__rw_allocate= unchecked. For =n= past =max_size ()= the product
wraps and a short block comes back, where 14882:2003 20.4.1.1, p5
returns storage of size ~n * sizeof(T)~ and p7 throws =bad_alloc= when
it cannot be obtained. The containers check =max_size= first; a direct
caller does not. =__rw_tmpbuf= checks its product (=src/tmpbuf.cpp=
64-70). Seen in passing by the extension sweep, 2026-11-04.

Fix: =allocate= throws =bad_alloc= for a count past =max_size ()=.

Check: ~std::allocator<int> ().allocate (std::allocator<int>
().max_size () + 1)~ throws =bad_alloc=.

** TODO =vector<bool>='s range constructor takes no allocator and no integers

14882:2003 23.2.5's synopsis declares the range constructor with
~const Allocator& = Allocator ()~, and 23.1.1, p9 has a range
constructor on an integral type behave as the count-and-value one.
=include/vector= 978-985 declare ~vector (InputIter first, InputIter
last)~ alone, with no integral dispatch: by reading,
~std::vector<bool> v (5, 1);~ picks the template and fails in
~std::distance (int, int)~. Seen in passing by the extension sweep,
2026-11-04.

Fix: the allocator parameter, and the integral dispatch the primary
=vector= has.

Check: ~std::vector<bool> v (5, 1)~ compiles and holds five =true=
elements, and the range constructor takes an allocator.

** TODO =vector<bool>='s range =insert= reads an input range twice

=include/vector.cc= 562 takes ~std::distance (first, last)~ over the
range and then copies it (565 or 574), so an input iterator range is
spent on the count and the copy reads nothing. 14882:2003 24.1.1, p3:
"Algorithms on input iterators should never attempt to pass through
the same iterator twice." Seen in passing by the extension sweep,
2026-11-04.

Fix: the range =insert= reads an input range once, into a temporary or
element by element.

Check: =insert= at =begin ()= from an =istream_iterator<bool>= over "1
0 1" inserts three elements.

** TODO =valarray::sum= adds with ~operator+~ from =T ()=

14882:2003 26.3.2.7, p2 computes =sum= "by applying ~operator+=~ to a
copy of an element of the array and all other elements".
=include/valarray= 167-169 return ~accumulate (begin, end, value_type
())~, which starts from =T ()= and applies ~operator+~: a =T= with
~+=~ and no ~+~ does not compile, and one whose =T ()= is not an
additive identity gives another sum. Seen in passing by the extension
sweep, 2026-11-04.

Fix: =sum= copies the first element and applies ~+=~ for the rest.

Check: =sum= of a =valarray= over a type with ~+=~ alone compiles, and
over one whose =T ()= is 1 gives the elements' sum.

** TODO A second =cv[g]= with one =gslice= may read its start twice

=_C_next_ind= at the end of its walk wraps, sets =_C_reset= and returns
=start=, and the next call clears the flag and returns =start= again
(=src/valarray.cpp= 40-77). ~valarray<T>::operator[] (const gslice&)
const~ (=include/valarray= 2117-2135) calls it =_C_ind_numb ()= times on
the caller's =gslice= and never takes the wrapping call, so the state
stays at the last index. Hypothesis, by reading and a hand trace over
a 2-by-3 =gslice=: a second ~cv[g]~ with the same =g= reads =start=
twice and drops the last element. The =gslice_array= paths take the
wrap in their loop condition and start clean. Seen in passing by the
extension sweep, 2026-11-04.

Fix: the const subscript steps a copy of the =gslice=, as
=gslice_array= does.

Check: ~a = cv[g]; b = cv[g];~ for a 2-by-3 =gslice=, with =a= and =b=
equal element by element.

** TODO The =random_shuffle= generator's warm-up may run four table entries

=src/random.cpp= 87-91: the warm-up loop indexes ~__rw_table [i]~
inside the loop over =ii=, so it runs entries 0 to 3 fifty-five times
each where the subtractive generator it follows runs all fifty-five
four times. The line is unchanged since the 2005 import. Hypothesis,
by reading: the inner index is meant to be =ii=. A fix moves every
result and =25.random.shuffle='s expected array. Seen in passing by
the extension sweep, 2026-11-04.

Fix: the inner loop runs over ~__rw_table [ii]~, then the test's
expected array.

Check: the loop read against Knuth's subtractive generator or
Numerical Recipes' =ran3=.

** TODO The standard objects are references, which a template argument can tell

=include/iostream= 55-62 declare the eight objects through
=_RWSTD_IOSTREAM_OBJECT= as references, and =src/iostream.cpp= 74-78
call the departure from 27.3 undetectable. 14882:2003 14.3.2, p1 takes
as a template argument the address of an object with external linkage.
Hypothesis, by reading: ~template <std::ostream&> struct S;~ cannot be
instantiated on =std::cout=, since a reference is not an object. Seen
in passing by the extension sweep, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: record the departure in chapter 2, or define the
objects as objects.

Check: compile ~S<std::cout>~.

** TODO =_C_opfx= skips the tie's flush on a loop of distinct buffers

=include/ostream= 270-284 skip ~tie ()->flush ()~ when the tie chain
loops or a stream in it shares the buffer. Footnote 292 of 14882:2003
27.6.2.3 allows the skip where no synchronization is necessary, which
holds for a shared buffer but not for a loop over distinct buffers.
The loop exists because =flush= builds a sentry (LWG 581); 2003's
27.6.2.6, p7 says =flush= does not behave as an unformatted output
function. Hypothesis, by reading: two streams on distinct buffers tied
to each other do not flush each other. Seen in passing by the
extension sweep, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: flush each distinct buffer of the loop once, or
record the skip.

Check: two streams on distinct buffers, tied to each other: an
insertion into one flushes the other.

** TODO =<stdlib.h>= and =<math.h>= leave the C++ overloads out of the global namespace

14882:2003 D.5, p2 has each =name.h= place the =cname= header's names
in the global namespace. =include/rw/_config.h= 97-106 defines
=_RWSTD_NO_DEPRECATED_C_HEADERS= unless a program asks for the pure
headers, and under it =include/ansi/stdlib.h= and =math.h= include the
C library's header and nothing more (=stdlib.h= 73-74). Hypothesis, by
reading: under glibc =<stdlib.h>= then gives no ~::abs (long)~ and
=<math.h>= no ~::sin (float)~, so ~abs (-1L)~ after =<stdlib.h>= calls
~abs (int)~. Seen in passing by the extension sweep, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: the =name.h= headers bring the =cname= overloads to
global scope, or record the C library's set.

Check: ~sizeof (abs (0L))~ after =<stdlib.h>= alone.

** TODO An installed =config.h= shares its name with the programs'

=include/rw/_config.h= 36 includes =<config.h>=, a name many programs'
build systems use, and =make install= copies the library's to the top
of =$(PREFIX)/include= (=GNUmakefile= 633). Hypothesis, by reading: a
program whose own =config.h= is earlier on the search path configures
the library with it, and one whose own is later gets the library's.
Seen in passing by the extension sweep, 2026-11-04.

Fix: a decision: a name of the library's own, under =rw/=, or record.

Check: ~#include <string>~ with =-I= of a directory holding an empty
=config.h= ahead of the library's.

** TODO =type_info= compares by name where GCC marks a name to compare by address

In 15D under GCC =_RWSTD_NO_TYPE_INFO_BEFORE=, =_EQUALITY= and =_NAME=
are defined (=config.h= 484-487), so =src/typeinfo.cpp= 87-130
compiles =before=, =name= and the comparisons. =name= returns the raw
mangled name and equality compares names with =strcmp=; GCC's support
library skips a leading =*=, its mark for a name to compare by
address. Hypothesis, by reading: =typeid= of an anonymous-namespace
type gives a =name ()= that starts with =*=, and two such types of one
name in two translation units compare equal. Seen in passing by the
extension sweep, 2026-11-04.

Fix: per the check: =name= skips the mark and a marked name compares
by address, as the support library does.

Check: those two calls in a two-file program.

** TODO The range =splice= checks 23.2.2.4 p12 with the wrong iterators

The range =splice= (=include/list.cc=) asserts
~__i != __j && __i != __k~ when the source is the list itself, and in
a debug build walks ~[__j, __k)~ asserting ~__i != __j~ at every
step, an invariant of the loop. 14882:2003 23.2.2.4 p12 leaves the
result undefined only for a position in ~[first, last)~: a position
inside the range past =first= is never caught, and a position equal
to =last=, which the paragraph allows and which changes nothing,
fails the assertion; a release build returns at once. From the 2005
import. Seen in passing while retiring the node buffer, 2026-11-04.

Fix: the loop asserts ~__i != __it~, and the first assertion drops
~__i != __k~.

Check: ~l.splice (l.end (), l, l.begin (), l.end ())~ runs in 15D; a
position inside the range fails the assertion.

** TODO The stream classes keep =_C_= members public

A census after =basic_istream='s helpers went protected (04c3bb3e):
=include/ostream= 69 =_C_ios_type=, 77 =_C_opfx=, 221 =_C_is_sync=
and 226 =_C_pad= are public; the first three have no user outside
=basic_ostream= and its sentry, and =_C_pad= is used by
=__rw::__rw_insert= (=include/rw/_ioinsert.cc= 150, 155).
=include/streambuf= 79 =_C_getloc= is public and used only by
=basic_filebuf= (=include/fstream.cc=), a derived class; 148 =_C_mode=
is public and used by ~basic_ostream::operator<< (basic_streambuf*)~
(=include/ostream.cc= 149), and =basic_ostream= is already a friend.
=include/rw/_iosbase.h= 231 =_C_getloc= is public and used by every
facet's =do_get= and =do_put= (=include/loc/_*.cc=), =complex.cc=,
=_ioinsert.cc= and =_stringio.cc= through an =ios_base&=; 303
=_C_usr_data= is public under a comment naming an EDG eccp 2.3x bug,
and EDG is on the matrix. =basic_streambuf= and =ios_base= derive
publicly from =__rw::__rw_synchronized=, so =_C_mutex=, =_C_lock=,
=_C_unlock= and =_C_guard= (=include/rw/_mutex.h=) are public members
of both, and =basic_ios::_C_bufmutex= reads ~rdbuf ()->_C_mutex~.
17.4.4.4, p2 permits added member function signatures a program cannot
tell from the standard ones, and a public reserved name a program can
call is one it can tell; 17.4.4.6, p1 leaves friendship unspecified,
so friends are within the latitude. Found on the review of 04c3bb3e,
2026-11-06.

Fix: in parts. =_C_ios_type=, =_C_opfx= and =_C_is_sync= go private;
=_C_pad= private with =__rw_insert= a friend; =streambuf='s =_C_getloc=
protected and =_C_mode= private. =ios_base::_C_getloc= is the wide
one: the facets reach it through =ios_base&=, so it is either a
friend per facet or a =__rw= accessor that is itself a friend, a
decision. =_C_usr_data= waits on whether EDG 6 still needs it, a
check in the EDG build. The =__rw_synchronized= base is a decision:
private inheritance with the stream classes' users made friends, or
the members protected in the base.

Check: the library builds; =27.ostream=, =27.basic.ios=, =27.objects=
and the 22 locale rows hold at pin.

** TODO =bitset= sizes its array with an enumerator in a boolean context

=include/bitset= 92 declares ~unsigned long _C_bits [_C_nelems ?
_C_nelems : 1]~, where =_C_nelems= is an enumerator. GCC 16 under
